    "case_id": "746",
    "case_summary": "An 80-year-old Japanese man presented with a 7-month history of cough. The cough was sometimes accompanied by white viscous sputum without bleeding, but was mostly dry. The cough would become paroxysmal without triggers, but would improve with periods of no symptoms. Medical history included myocardial infarction at age 68 with percutaneous coronary intervention and stenting. Former smoker (2 packs/day for 48 years, quit at age 68). No remarkable family history. Lives with wife without neurological challenges. Patient recalled a choking episode while eating yellowtail fish approximately 11 months prior to presentation.\n\nVital signs on admission:- Temperature: 35.9 °C- Blood pressure: 126/64 mmHg- Heart rate: 70 beats/min- Oxygen saturation: 97% (room air)\n\n{'Laboratory Tests': '- White blood cell count: 8590/μL (neutrophil 70.5%)- Red blood cell count: 455 × 104/μL- Hemoglobin: 14.1 g/dL- Platelet: 18.4/μL- AST: 18 U/L- ALT: 13 U/L- ALP: 219 U/L- γ-GTP: 13 U/L- LDH: 176 U/L- Creatinine: 1.12 mg/dL- BUN: 20 mg/dL- Other markers: CYFRA 1.7 ng/mL, ProGRP 49.9 pg/mL, CEA 2.0 ng/mL, CA19-9 17.7 U/mL, PSA 1.401 ng/mL', 'Imaging Studies': \"1. Chest Radiograph:- Title: Chest X-ray- Description: Showed slight increase in opacity in the right lower lung2. Chest CT:- Title: Chest computed tomography- Description: Revealed obstructive pneumonia surrounding edematous area in the right lower lung and a high-density structure3. Bronchoscopy:- Title: Flexible bronchoscopy- Description: Showed obstruction of the basal segmental bronchus's lumen with edematous formation in the right lower lung\"}",
